<section>
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div class="pic"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1749553045991-e9d6988d3974?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="woman in a long blue Victorian-style dress standing in a field" width="800" height="880" loading="lazy"></div>
    <div>
      <span class="eb">Then &amp; now</span>
      <h2>From steel hoops to soft tulle</h2>
      <p>In the 1850s, the cage crinoline let fashionable skirts grow wider than ever without layers of heavy petticoats. A century later, net petticoats gave 1950s dresses their famous swing.</p>
      <p>Today you&#8217;ll find volume in bridal gowns, prom dresses, swing-dance outfits and playful everyday tulle skirts. Read the full story in our <a href="crinoline-history.html">crinoline history</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap faq"><span class="eb">Questions</span><h2 style="margin-bottom:20px">Full skirt FAQ</h2><details open><summary>What is a crinoline?</summary><p>Originally, crinoline was a stiff fabric woven from horsehair and linen, used to make petticoats that held skirts out. By the late 1850s the word also described the cage crinoline, a light frame of steel hoops worn under very full skirts.</p></details><details><summary>What&#8217;s the difference between a petticoat and a crinoline?</summary><p>A petticoat is any underskirt, often made of layers of net or tulle, that adds softness and volume. A crinoline, in its classic form, is a structured hoop frame that creates a wider, more rigid shape.</p></details><details><summary>How many petticoats do I need for a 1950s look?</summary><p>One full net petticoat gives a gentle lift; two layered petticoats create the classic swing-dance bell shape. Choose a petticoat a little shorter than your skirt so it doesn&#8217;t peek out.</p></details><details><summary>Can I sit comfortably in a hoop skirt?</summary><p>Yes, with practice. Most modern hoops are flexible: lift the back of the hoop slightly as you sit so it folds rather than flips up at the front.</p></details><details><summary>How do I store a tulle petticoat?</summary><p>Hang it upside down from the waistband, or store it loosely in a large breathable bag. Avoid squashing it under heavy items, which flattens the volume.</p></details></div></section>

